<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alfonso II of Aragon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1157">1157</a>–1196</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Alfonso the Chaste</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alfonso II was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-berenguer-iv-count-of-barcelona">Ramon Berenguer IV of Barcelona</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petronilla-of-aragon">Petronila of Aragon</a>. He became Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in 1162 and King of Aragon in 1164, the first ruler to hold both by inheritance, uniting them in what became the Crown of Aragon. He secured Provence for his family, extended Aragon south against the Muslims, founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teruel">Teruel</a> and was a patron of troubadour poetry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfonso-ii-of-aragon.jpg" alt="Alfonso II of Aragon" width="202" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknow · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alfonso_II_de_Arag%C3%B3n_from_Liber_feudorum_maior.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Alfonso was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huesca">Huesca</a> and was known from birth as both Alfonso and Ramon. He took the name Alfonso on the united throne of Aragon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in deference to the Aragonese, to honour Alfonso the Battler. For most of his reign he was allied with King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> and against the Moorish taifas of the south, although the Crown of Aragon also owed Castile a formal bond of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a>. In 1171 he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teruel">Teruel</a>, an important stronghold on the road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspe">Caspe</a> in the same year.</p><p>The Treaty of Sangüesa of 1168 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-vi-of-navarre">Sancho VI of Navarre</a> divided the territory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">Taifa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a> between them. The Treaty of Cazorla of 1179 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> marked out zones of conquest in the south along the watershed of the rivers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jucar">Júcar</a> and Segura and secured the south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denia">Denia</a>, for Aragon. On 18 January 1174 he married Sancha of Castile at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Under Alfonso, Catalan-Aragonese influence north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> reached its height. His realms took in Provence, held from 1166 or just before, the county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdanya">Cerdanya</a> in 1168 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roussillon">Roussillon</a>, inherited in 1172. Béarn and Bigorre paid homage to him in 1187. In 1186 he supported his cousin Agalbursa in placing her grandson Hugh on the throne of Arborea in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>. His involvement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a>, which would later cost the life of his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-ii-of-aragon">Peter II of Aragon</a>, strengthened Aragonese trade and encouraged migration from the north to the newly reconquered lands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Alfonso gave the first land grant to Cistercian monks on the banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> in Aragon, and the Monasterio de Piedra was founded in 1194 with thirteen monks from Poblet Monastery. He also patronised the Carthusians and founded the first chapterhouse of Scala Dei in 1196. He was a noted poet and a close friend of King Richard the Lionheart. A tensó attributed to him and Giraut de Bornelh debates whether a lady is dishonoured by taking a lover richer than herself, and many troubadours mention him. He died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perpignan">Perpignan</a> in 1196, and his son Peter II succeeded him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alfonso II of Aragon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their son <b>Alfonso II</b> succeeded in Barcelona in 1162 and in Aragon in 1164, and the county became the core of the Crown of Aragon.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-barcelona">County of Barcelona</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1192 Countess Dolça bequeathed the county to <b>Alfonso II of Aragon</b>, who incorporated it into the Crown of Aragon.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-pallars-jussa">County of Pallars Jussà</a></li><li><blockquote>The last count, Girard II, died without heirs in 1172 and left Roussillon to <b>Alfonso II of Aragon</b> and Barcelona.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-roussillon">County of Roussillon</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huesca">Huesca</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perpignan">Perpignan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.14,-0.41&amp;year=1182" aria-label="Huesca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Alfonso II of Aragon"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4974 3969h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4974 3969h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.14,-0.41&amp;year=1182">What was here in 1182? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
